    "rawResponseText": "**Hotel branded residences** are privately owned homes—such as condos, villas, or penthouses—developed in partnership with a hotel or luxury lifestyle brand, so buyers get hotel-style services, design standards, and brand recognition.[5][7]\n\nCommon features include **concierge**, housekeeping, valet, in-residence dining, wellness amenities, and sometimes shared access to a hotel’s restaurants, spas, and pools.[5][6][7]\n\nThere are usually **two main models**:\n- **Hotel-integrated residences**: attached to or adjacent to a hotel, often sharing some amenities with hotel guests.[5][7]\n- **Standalone branded residences**: branded homes without a hotel on-site, focused on exclusive residential use.[5][7][8]\n\nWhy they appeal to buyers:\n- They offer a **turnkey luxury lifestyle** with managed services and less day-to-day maintenance.[3][5][6]\n- They often carry a **price premium** over comparable non-branded homes, reflecting the brand and service level.[4][7]\n- Developers and hotel brands use them to expand into real estate while diversifying revenue.[3][6]\n\nExamples of brands active in this space include **Marriott**, **Hilton**, **Accor**, **Ritz-Carlton**, **Four Seasons**, and **St. Regis**.[1][6][8][9]\n\nIf you want, I can also explain **how branded residences differ from condos**, **investment pros and cons**, or **top hotel-branded residence brands by region**.",
